## Topic :-
To develop a C++ program to store a tournament bracket in an array, compute the parent, left child, and right child of any given index, and display the tournament tree level by level.

## Problem Statement :-
To develop a program for efficient representation and management of a tournament bracket using an array. The program should determine the parent node and its left and right child nodes for a specified index. It should also arrange and display the tournament structure level by level using the concepts of a binary tree, making it easier to understand the relationships between nodes and the progression of tournament rounds.

## Objective :-
1.	To represent a tournament bracket using an array.
2.	To understand the array representation of a binary tree.
3.	To find the parent, left child, and right child of any given index.
4.	To display the tournament tree level by level.
5.	To implement basic tree operations using C++.
6.	To understand the practical application of tree data structures in tournament management.

## Introduction :-
A tournament bracket can be represented using a binary tree, in which each node represents a player or a match. An array is a simple way to store the elements of a binary tree without using pointers. For an element at index i, its parent is at index (i-1)/2, its left child is at index 2i+1, and its right child is at index 2i+2 when using zero-based indexing. The tree can be displayed level by level by traversing the array in order.

## Algorithm :-
1.	Start.
2.	Declare an array to store the tournament bracket.
3.	Accept the number of elements from the user.
4.	Store the tournament bracket elements in the array.
5.	Accept the index for which the parent and children are to be calculated.
6.	If the index is valid, calculate the parent, left child, and right child.
7.	Display the parent and children if they exist.
8.	Display the tree level by level.
9.	Stop.

  ## Result :-
  The program was successfully implemented to represent a tournament bracket using an array. It correctly calculates the parent, left child, and right child of a given index using binary tree formulas. It also checks whether the given index is valid and displays appropriate messages when a parent or child does not exist. The tree elements are displayed level by level, helping to understand the hierarchical structure of a binary tree. Thus, the objectives of the practical were achieved successfully.

  ## Conclusion :-
  In this practical, we learned how to represent a binary tree using an array and perform basic tree operations. We understood the relationship between parent and child nodes using array indices. We also learned how to calculate the left and right children and identify the parent of a particular node. The level by level display helped us understand the structure and arrangement of nodes in a tournament bracket. This practical improved our understanding of array-based tree representation and its applications in Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA).
  
  
  

